description:
You are given the reference pages of a small string library, modelled on Python's string methods. Implement every function so that it matches its page **exactly**. The tests check each edge case the pages describe, and nothing they do not.

```cpp
std::vector<std::string> splitWhitespace(std::string_view s, int maxSplit = -1);
std::vector<std::string> split(std::string_view s, std::string_view sep, int maxSplit = -1);
std::string join(const std::vector<std::string>& parts, std::string_view sep);
std::string replaceAll(std::string_view s, std::string_view from, std::string_view to, int maxCount = -1);
bool startsWith(std::string_view s, std::string_view prefix, std::size_t start = 0);
std::vector<std::string> splitLines(std::string_view s, bool keepEnds = false);
```

**Whitespace** in this library means the six characters space, `\t`, `\n`, `\r`, `\f` and `\v`.

**splitWhitespace** splits `s` into words separated by runs of whitespace. Leading and trailing whitespace is ignored, so an empty or all-whitespace string gives an **empty list**. If `maxSplit` is zero or more, at most `maxSplit` splits are made: after that many words, the rest of the string, with its leading whitespace removed but its trailing whitespace kept, is the last element. A negative `maxSplit` means no limit.

**split** splits `s` at every occurrence of `sep`, from the left. Consecutive separators produce empty strings between them, and a separator at either end produces an empty string there; an empty `s` gives a list holding one empty string. At most `maxSplit` splits are made when it is zero or more; the rest of the string is the last element. **Throws** `std::invalid_argument` if `sep` is empty.

**join** returns the parts with `sep` between each pair; no parts give an empty string.

**replaceAll** returns a copy of `s` with occurrences of `from` replaced by `to`, found from the left without overlapping; if `maxCount` is zero or more, only the first `maxCount` are replaced. **If `from` is empty**, it matches at the start of `s`, between every two characters and at the end, so `replaceAll("abc", "", "-")` is `"-a-b-c-"` and with `maxCount` 2 it is `"-a-bc"`.

**startsWith** returns whether the part of `s` beginning at index `start` begins with `prefix`. If `start` is greater than `s.size()` the result is false, even for an empty prefix; if it equals `s.size()`, only an empty prefix matches.

**splitLines** splits `s` at line boundaries, which in this library are `\n`, `\r` and the pair `\r\n` (one boundary). A line ending at the very end of `s` does not create an extra empty line, so `"a\n"` gives one line and the empty string gives none. If `keepEnds` is true, each line keeps its boundary characters.

